# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# @sip/web — configuration
#
#   cp packages/website-oficial/.env.example packages/website-oficial/.env.local
#
# HOW THESE ARE READ
# The server reads them at REQUEST time (src/lib/config.ts, called from the
# /wallets server component and from the route handlers) and passes only the
# browser-safe subset to the client as props. Nothing is inlined at build time,
# so ONE Docker image runs unchanged locally, on a VPS and on Railway: changing
# the RPC endpoint is a restart, not a rebuild.
#
# NOTE FOR LOCAL RUNS: `next dev` loads this file automatically. The production
# standalone server (what the container runs) does NOT — and .dockerignore keeps
# .env files out of the image on purpose — so a deployment must supply real
# environment variables.
#
# NAMING: the canonical names are the NUVEM_* ones `docker-compose.yml` already
# uses. Every NUVEM_* name also accepts a SIP_* spelling, and the NEXT_PUBLIC_* /
# plain aliases listed below still resolve, so an existing .env keeps working. The dashboard at / reads none of this; only
# /wallets and the API routes do.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- REQUIRED ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Privy App ID.  aliases: PRIVY_APP_ID, SIP_PRIVY_APP_ID, NEXT_PUBLIC_PRIVY_APP_ID
# Public by design (it identifies your app, it authorises nothing). What actually
# stops someone else using it is the Allowed-origins list in the Privy dashboard,
# so treat that list as a security control and fill it in.
#
#   1. https://dashboard.privy.io -> Create app -> Web platform. Copy the App ID
#      (a short opaque string, looks like cm…, exactly 25 chars — the server
#      checks the length because Privy 500s every page on any other).
#   2. Configuration -> Login methods -> ENABLE "Wallet". Skip this and the login
#      modal opens with no wallet option and no console error — it just looks
#      broken.
#   3. Configuration -> App settings -> Domains -> Allowed origins: add
#      http://localhost:3002 (the port is mandatory, Privy will not infer it),
#      and later your production origin. https required for non-localhost.
#   4. Embedded wallets -> execution mode: TEE ("user-controlled server
#      wallets"). Importing a trading wallet with a seat only works in TEE
#      mode; on-device mode attaches no signer and the page says so.
PRIVY_APP_ID=

# Robinhood Chain (chainId 4663) JSON-RPC endpoint.
#   aliases: NUVEM_RPC_URL, SIP_RPC_URL, RPC_URL, RPC_URL_4663
#
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY. Every contract read runs in a route handler against this URL,
# so it may safely carry an API key — it is never sent to the browser. Verify that
# claim yourself with:  curl -s localhost:3002/wallets | grep -c '<your-api-key>'  -> 0
#
# NOT AN ALCHEMY FREE-TIER KEY. That tier caps eth_getLogs at a 10-BLOCK range,
# and this page recovers a vault's trading wallets with ONE unchunked getLogs
# from NUVEM_LOGS_FROM_BLOCK to the head. Under the cap that call fails, and the
# page can only report the wallet list as unknown. Pay-As-You-Go on chain 4663
# answers it; the worker needs the same plan, plus archive, for its own reasons.
NUVEM_RPC_URL=

# VaultFactory.  aliases: NUVEM_VAULT_FACTORY, SIP_VAULT_FACTORY, VAULT_FACTORY, NEXT_PUBLIC_VAULT_FACTORY
# REQUIRED. There is no default, on purpose. It is the ONLY address the app
# needs — the executor, WETH, pause controller and attester registry are all read
# from VaultFactory.protocolConfiguration() at runtime, because PersonalVault only
# accepts component addresses the factory approves — and it is also the only one
# that CANNOT be cross-checked against the chain, because it is the question the
# chain is being asked. A default here went stale twice: the site created vaults
# on a superseded deployment while the worker, pinned to the current one, reported
# no wallets and looked idle rather than misconfigured.
#
# THE FACTORY MUST BE THE ONE THIS BUILD WAS COMPILED AGAINST. Its
# ProtocolConfiguration has four fields; an older deployment's had six. The
# server refuses to decode a six-word answer rather than silently reading the
# addresses out of the wrong slots, so pointing this at an old factory produces
# "could not be read" on the create-vault card, not a mis-wired vault.
#
# LEFT BLANK BECAUSE SIP HAS NO DEPLOYMENT YET, and the forked project's factory
# is abandoned rather than inherited: the trading accounts under it carry a
# savings rate that meant a percentage of PROFIT, while SIP's rate is basis
# points of VOLUME. A vault created against that factory would be skimmed on a
# profit-era rate applied to a notional — about a hundred times the intent — and
# a vault cannot be moved once it exists. Deploy SIP's own factory and set this.
NUVEM_VAULT_FACTORY=

# --- THE SEAT (born-seated trading wallets) ----------------------------------

# The app's authorization-key SIGNER and the POLICY that bounds it.
#   aliases: PRIVY_SIGNER_ID / SIP_PRIVY_SIGNER_ID / NEXT_PUBLIC_PRIVY_SIGNER_ID
#            PRIVY_POLICY_ID / SIP_PRIVY_POLICY_ID / NEXT_PUBLIC_PRIVY_POLICY_ID
#
# Every trading wallet the page creates is minted with this signer already
# seated, under this policy, so the worker can pull the skim without ever
# holding the wallet's key. BOTH OR NEITHER: a signer with no policy is FULL
# permission at Privy, so the server refuses half a pair as a configuration
# problem rather than letting it degrade. Both unset means no seats — the page
# says so on every wallet. They are two different 25-character ids; pasting
# the signer id into the policy variable is refused by name, because it once
# ran that way in production and failed with one opaque "Invalid policy ID".
#
# THE POLICY PINS THE EXECUTOR'S ADDRESS — it allows `settle` to that contract
# and nothing else, allows `invest` only with value 0, and denies every key
# export. So deploying a new executor means updating the policy to the new
# address before any pull can be signed under it.
PRIVY_SIGNER_ID=
PRIVY_POLICY_ID=

# --- RECOMMENDED -------------------------------------------------------------

# Block explorer base URL, no trailing path.
#   aliases: NUVEM_EXPLORER_URL, SIP_EXPLORER_URL, NEXT_PUBLIC_EXPLORER_URL, EXPLORER_URL_4663
# Used for links, and surfaced by MetaMask when it is asked to add chain 4663.
NUVEM_EXPLORER_URL=

# L2 block to start log scans from when discovering trading wallets.
#   aliases: NUVEM_LOGS_FROM_BLOCK, SIP_LOGS_FROM_BLOCK, LOGS_FROM_BLOCK
# PersonalVault exposes activeTradingAccountCount() but NO enumeration, so
# wallets are recovered from TradingAccountInvited / Activated / Revoked logs.
# Set it to the block NUVEM_VAULT_FACTORY was deployed at. Default 0, which
# scans from genesis: slow at best, and refused outright by any endpoint that
# caps the eth_getLogs range. A failed scan is reported on the page as
# "unknown", never as an empty list. Blank until there is a deployment to
# name a block in.
NUVEM_LOGS_FROM_BLOCK=

# The worker's Postgres, read by GET /api/skims for the put-aside / collected /
# pending figures per wallet.  aliases: DATABASE_URL, SIP_DATABASE_URL, NUVEM_DATABASE_URL
# Optional: without it the skim status renders "Status unavailable" and the
# rest of the page is unaffected. Server-side only.
DATABASE_URL=

# --- OPTIONAL ----------------------------------------------------------------

# The RPC URL handed to the USER'S WALLET, embedded in the chain object MetaMask
# receives via wallet_addEthereumChain for a chain it has never seen.
#   aliases: NUVEM_PUBLIC_RPC_URL, SIP_PUBLIC_RPC_URL, NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL, NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL_4663
#
# Leave it UNSET and the app gives the wallet its own same-origin relay,
# <your-origin>/api/rpc, which forwards a small allowlist of methods upstream,
# rate-limits each client to 60 requests a minute, and keeps NUVEM_RPC_URL's API
# key on the server. If you would rather not expose a relay at all, set this to
# a key-free public endpoint for chain 4663 — doing so switches the relay off
# automatically. Its origin is added to the CSP's connect-src for you.
#
# Whatever you put here IS visible to the user. Never point it at a URL
# containing an API key.
NUVEM_PUBLIC_RPC_URL=

# Set to 1 to refuse to relay at /api/rpc. Wallets then cannot auto-add chain
# 4663 unless NUVEM_PUBLIC_RPC_URL is set, and the user must add it by hand.
#   aliases: NUVEM_DISABLE_RPC_PROXY, SIP_DISABLE_RPC_PROXY
NUVEM_DISABLE_RPC_PROXY=

# Upgrade cohort a NEW vault joins.  aliases: NUVEM_COHORT_ID, SIP_COHORT_ID, COHORT_ID
# Mainnet uses 1. Validated against VaultFactory.cohorts(id), and the whole
# createVault call is simulated, before anything is signed.
NUVEM_COHORT_ID=1

# Chain id, purely as an assertion.  aliases: NUVEM_CHAIN_ID, SIP_CHAIN_ID, NEXT_PUBLIC_CHAIN_ID, CHAIN_ID
# This build targets 4663 only; any other value is reported as a configuration
# error rather than silently ignored.
NUVEM_CHAIN_ID=4663

# Privy App Client ID.  aliases: PRIVY_CLIENT_ID, SIP_PRIVY_CLIENT_ID, NEXT_PUBLIC_PRIVY_CLIENT_ID
# Only needed if you created an App Client to separate dev/staging/prod.
PRIVY_CLIENT_ID=

# --- OPTIONAL CROSS-CHECKS ---------------------------------------------------
# This app NEVER prefers these over what the factory reports — they exist only
# so the UI can say "your environment disagrees with the chain", which is how a
# stale .env gets caught instead of quietly misleading you.
#
# ALL FOUR ARE BLANK, and they stay blank until SIP's contracts are deployed:
# there is nothing to expect yet, and the previous values belonged to the
# abandoned deployment described under NUVEM_VAULT_FACTORY. Once you have your
# own, read them back from that factory's protocolConfiguration() and paste them
# here; the factory's answer remains the authority in every case.
#   aliases: NUVEM_SETTLEMENT_EXECUTOR / SIP_SETTLEMENT_EXECUTOR / NEXT_PUBLIC_SETTLEMENT_EXECUTOR / EXECUTOR
#            NUVEM_WETH / SIP_WETH / NEXT_PUBLIC_WETH / WETH
#            NUVEM_PAUSE_CONTROLLER / SIP_PAUSE_CONTROLLER / NEXT_PUBLIC_PAUSE_CONTROLLER / PAUSE_CONTROLLER
#            NUVEM_ATTESTER_REGISTRY / SIP_ATTESTER_REGISTRY / NEXT_PUBLIC_ATTESTER_REGISTRY / ATTESTER_REGISTRY
NUVEM_SETTLEMENT_EXECUTOR=
NUVEM_WETH=
NUVEM_PAUSE_CONTROLLER=
NUVEM_ATTESTER_REGISTRY=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DELIBERATELY NOT READ BY THIS WAVE
#
# PRIVY_APP_SECRET is server-only and belongs to the routes that talk to
# Privy's server API on the user's behalf (re-seating a wallet, the worker's
# pulls). Nothing in src/lib/config.ts reads it, so nothing here can leak it;
# when a route needs it, that route reads it at request time and it must never
# carry a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
#
# No attester or worker key belongs here either. This app performs writes only
# through the user's own wallets — the pension key signs createVault and
# invites, a trading wallet signs its own acceptance, rate and revocation.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# =============================================================================
# SOLANA (SIP_CHAIN=solana)
#
# One image serves both chains. SIP_CHAIN picks one at REQUEST time, so a flip is
# a restart, not a rebuild. Under SIP_CHAIN=solana every EVM variable above is
# IGNORED (it may stay set; it is not refused), so switching back is a restart
# too. The EVM API routes (/api/rpc, /api/vault, /api/create-vault,
# /api/dashboard, /api/skims) then answer 404, and /api/solana-rpc and
# /api/solana-tx answer instead.
#
# REFUSED, NOT ALIASED: every NUVEM_SOLANA_* name (NUVEM_SOLANA_RPC_URL,
# NUVEM_SOLANA_PROGRAM_ID, NUVEM_SOLANA_PUBLIC_WS_URL, …). A copied Nuvem
# environment is a configuration problem naming the SIP_SOLANA_* replacement,
# because Nuvem's program key leaked. So is Nuvem's program id itself, and so are
# the keeper's signing-secret names (SIP_SOLANA_SETTLE_KEY,
# SIP_SOLANA_PRIVY_APP_SECRET, SIP_SOLANA_PRIVY_AUTHORIZATION_KEY): the web never
# signs and must not hold them. The check is by NAME, so do not leave any of them
# here even blank.
# =============================================================================

# evm (the default when unset) or solana.
SIP_CHAIN=

# REQUIRED under solana. The web's own Helius JSON-RPC URL(s), comma-separated,
# tried in order. SERVER-SIDE ONLY, and it carries an API key: it is never
# returned in a response, logged, placed in the CSP or handed to Privy. Nothing is
# appended implicitly; list a second provider yourself if you have one. Use a
# different key from the keeper's.
SIP_SOLANA_RPC_URLS=

# REQUIRED under solana. An assertion, not the source: it must equal the address
# in packages/solana-program/idl/sip_vault.json, which is what the code uses.
# Nuvem's 7rtgXTu852M1NTx7PLoJd3bChaCb2hgsgv5o54aFv6Fy is refused by name.
SIP_SOLANA_PROGRAM_ID=6kA9H9zQT6PW5xWkXoAFCS3NotxarzaYqj66mjMf9w4J

# REQUIRED under solana. The ONE header the edge in front of this process writes
# from the connection. The per-client limits key on it and ignore every other
# header; a request without it shares one bucket. On Railway, Envoy writes
# x-envoy-external-address (confirm by echoing headers on the deployed service).
# x-forwarded-for, forwarded and x-client-ip are refused: a client writes those
# itself. Set it under evm too, to put /api/rpc and /api/vault on the same rule;
# unset, those keep their older list of edge headers.
SIP_TRUSTED_CLIENT_IP_HEADER=

# Optional. The key-free Solana WebSocket handed to the browser (Privy's
# rpcSubscriptions), and the only Solana origin in the CSP. Default
# wss://api.mainnet-beta.solana.com. A wss:// origin only: a path, a query,
# credentials, or the host of any SIP_SOLANA_RPC_URLS entry is refused, because
# that is where a key hides. The browser's HTTP RPC has no variable: it is always
# this app's own /api/solana-rpc.
SIP_SOLANA_PUBLIC_WS_URL=

# Optional, both or neither. The solana-keeper's Privy signer id, and the Solana
# policy (chain_type solana) that bounds it, seated on trading wallets by the
# wallet screens. Ids, not keys. PRIVY_SIGNER_ID and PRIVY_POLICY_ID are not read
# under solana: an EVM policy cannot bound a Solana wallet.
SIP_SOLANA_PRIVY_SIGNER_ID=
SIP_SOLANA_PRIVY_POLICY_ID=

# Optional budgets, in weighted tokens per minute (weights: @sip/solana-core
# relay-policy.ts). In memory and per process, which is right for the single web
# replica. Blank means the default.
#   SIP_SOLANA_RELAY_PER_MIN                 per client, /api/solana-rpc (default 60, minimum 30)
#   SIP_SOLANA_RELAY_SIGNING_GLOBAL_PER_MIN  whole process, wallet-signing methods (default 1800)
#   SIP_SOLANA_RELAY_READS_GLOBAL_PER_MIN    whole process, account reads (default 1800)
#   SIP_SOLANA_SEND_PER_MIN                  per client, /api/solana-tx (default 6)
#   SIP_SOLANA_SEND_GLOBAL_PER_MIN           whole process, verified sends only (default 150)
# SIP_SOLANA_RELAY_GLOBAL_PER_MIN is refused by name: it was split into the two
# global budgets above, so reads can never starve signing.
#
# WHAT IT TAKES TO DENY EVERYONE: a global budget divided by its per-client limit
# is the number of client identities that, each spending its whole allowance,
# empty that budget for every visitor. A client is one IPv4 address or one IPv6
# /64; every IPv4 /24 and IPv6 /48 also shares one bucket of 4 x the per-client
# limit, so a home /56 or a tunnel /48 counts as at most 4 clients. The defaults
# keep the ratio at 25 or more (30 for the relay, 25 for sends). Change a limit
# only in the direction that keeps it there. Size the two relay globals together
# against the Helius plan: requests per second x 60 (a batch member is one request
# upstream, whatever it weighs here). Fixed in code: at most 32 relays in flight
# at once (6 per client); past that the relay answers 503 or 429 at once.
SIP_SOLANA_RELAY_PER_MIN=
SIP_SOLANA_RELAY_SIGNING_GLOBAL_PER_MIN=
SIP_SOLANA_RELAY_READS_GLOBAL_PER_MIN=
SIP_SOLANA_SEND_PER_MIN=
SIP_SOLANA_SEND_GLOBAL_PER_MIN=
